    "document_summary": "The Housing Price Prediction Model project is in the development stage, managed by Eljo Example, with Jordan Lee as the primary contact, and reviewed by Moe Example within the Economic Analysis Division. It uses classical supervised machine learning to predict residential property prices, training models from scratch using internal historical housing market data. The project utilizes open-source Python libraries but explicitly states it does not use generative AI, large language models, AI agents, information retrieval, fine-tuning, third-party models, external pretrained models, or foundation models. While the project uses personal data, it does not use sensitive or confidential data, nor third-party data. The system produces numeric price predictions, not free-text, and the team is reviewing potential linkage of record-level predictions to identifiable information. It is planned for internal operational deployment, requires human oversight before predictions are used, and may support analytical decisions. The project does not interact directly with the public or external clients. Governance considerations include documenting limitations, assessing proxy variables for protected characteristics, reviewing re-identification risks, and defining monitoring and retraining procedures.",
